# Tool input validation

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Issue | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
| Status | Draft |

This contract defines the registry boundary between model-supplied input and tool execution.

## Acceptance contract

- "'`execute` validates required fields, declared types and enum constraints before invoking an executor, returns a descriptive `ToolResponse` on rejection, identifies property paths and constraints without exposing input values in results or logs, passes accepted input including unknown keys unchanged exactly once without coercion or defaults, and preserves the unknown-tool exception.
- `register` rejects an uncompilable static schema with the tool name in the error and compiles accepted static schemas at registration for reuse across ten executions.
- Function-valued definitions resolve on every execution, validate using the current schema, and reuse compiled validators by resolved schema object identity.

## Integration and release obligations

The existing tool loop consumes rejection responses as ordinary tool results, allowing a corrected retry within its five-round budget; executor failures remain outside this input-validation contract.
The implementation uses a JSON Schema validator in runtime dependencies, with no opt-out flag.
README.md, docs/adding-a-tool.md and .specify/spec.md describe validation, extra-key preservation and rejection as a tool result; the execute comment is one line linking here.
A MINOR release and consumer-facing changelog explain that executors no longer receive malformed inputs, including those they previously handled themselves.
